 the owner called startRecoveryForInstance(0,0) and after the three PrepareReplies from replicas 2, 3 and 4 were delivered - shows the owner's instance at status PREACCEPTED with an empty command list (inst_cmds_len 0) and its bookkeeping at lb_status 1, lb_cmds_len 0, lb_appended 1, lb_nacks 0, lb_preparing true, lb_last_tried_ballot 0 and lb_ballot -1. Index 5 records that the owner emitted no PreAccept at all (proposed_cmd_op -1) and that its instance ended with no command; indices 10 and 11 repeat the same values for the second delivery order, so the outcome does not depend on the order of the equally admissible replies. The unresolved part is the mechanism: the single appended reply should be the self reply that startRecoveryForInstance built from status ACCEPTED and command X (epaxos/epaxos.go:1145-1165), which would leave lb.cmds non-empty and lb.status at ACCEPTED, and the three remote replies carry ballots equal to lastTriedBallot, so my reading of handlePrepareReply says they must either be appended or counted as nacks - neither of which the recorded lb_appended 1 and lb_nacks 0 show. Because the row's fields and the bookkeeping are mutually inconsistent with that reading, the honest state of this question is that the observation is retained (the instance lost its command in both orders) while its cause is undetermined: it could be a target path I have not traced, or a construction error in this probe (for instance the owner's instance object not being the one the probe reads), and the two must be separated before any claim is made. The planned discriminator is a re-run that records the owner's instance pointer identity, status and command length plus lb fields after each individual reply delivery, and that also re-reads startRecoveryForInstance's field order against the runtime values.\\\",\n  \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n   \\\"The outcome is order-independent in this run, which refutes the provisional hypothesis that the adopted value depends on arrival order among same-ballot replies: the last-adopted reply theory cannot explain an identical result in both orders.\\\",\n   \\\"The recorded lb_nacks 0 and lb_appended 1 are inconsistent with the mismatch branch I verified in the source and with the number of delivered replies, so the observation may reflect the probe's own construction rather than the target.\\\",\n   \\\"The instance state recorded at index 0 (status ACCEPTED with X) and at index 4 (PREACCEPTED with an empty command list) is a transition the traced writers do not produce, which is the same combination that the status/command invariant behavior rules out.\\\"],\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\n   \\\"The probe's own construction is not yet validated: it reads InstanceSpace[0][0] for the owner's instance and delivers replies through the ordinary handler path, but it does not record pointer identity or per-delivery bookkeeping, so an error in the probe cannot be excluded.\\\",\n   \\\"No other receipt in this run shows the owner's own recovery of its own instance, so there is no independent observation of the same scenario.\\\",\n   \\\"Only two delivery orders were exercised, both within one configuration (N=5, F=2, instance (0,0), one command).\\\"],\n  \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"All cited indices belong to receipt 81a44ff62122417db3cc5697fc5303f6 for fixed experiment 0757162a4aa64ef4a58a90002641eb27-experiment: index 0 is the owner's pre-recovery state, indices 2-3 the acceptors' states, index 4 the owner's bookkeeping after the recovery replies, index 5 the observed outcome, and indices 10-11 the same for the second delivery order. The two orders are separate runs of the same test function inside one execution.\\\"}]}\njson.dump(upd,open('draft/update28.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('written')\nPY\ntimeout 120 /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli impo